{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>An oracle concerning the beasts of the Negev, etc.</b> Joined [to the preceding], concerning those who go down to Egypt. And behold, the prophet speaks concerning the beasts of the Negev, and it is Egypt, for it is on the southern side opposite the land of Israel. And he said that on the road by which those who go down travel, they will encounter fierce beasts such as bears and leopards; therefore לביא and ליש are among them." "<b>In a land of distress.</b> That they cannot flee. And those who go down will carry their wealth upon the shoulder of donkeys, to flee with it to Egypt, like את החיל הזה (Deuteronomy 8:18)." "<b>דבשת.</b> Like גבנון, and it has no [such] meaning. And behold, the meaning is that the beasts of the Negev will destroy some of them."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {1 "The comparison to Deut. 8:18 is Ibn Ezra's cross-reference; the linkage is not immediately obvious and may involve an inferred verbal or thematic parallel.", 2 "Reading 'ואין רע לו' as Ibn Ezra's grammatical note that דבשת has no negative connotation; the syntax is compressed and the argument turns on a lexical point that remains terse."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:55:59.582542104Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}